Editing hub scripts with type checking
======================================

lua-language-server reads the definitions in ptokax-api.lua and gives
completion, signatures and type errors for the hub API. No plugin, no server
to write. Any editor with an LSP client works.

  ptokax-api.lua        generated definitions, do not edit
  gen-api-defs.lua      regenerates them from scripting-interface.txt
  luarc.json.example    LuaLS - Look up "workspace.library LuaLS"


Setup
-----

It's cleanest to put this in your nvim config folder, imo. But check your
runtime path. I just copy this tree directly into ~/.config/nvim/. Hence,
the json has the workspace path named accordingly. The .luarc.json is named
so it won't be hidden by default. Rename before moving.

You can just change the workspace.library path yourself, as well.

~/.config/nvim
├── lua
│   └── pxapi
│       ├── gen-api-defs.lua
│       └── ptokax-api.lua
├── README
└── .luarc.json


Event functions need annotating
-------------------------------

The server does not take parameter types from the declared global, so tUser is
unknown until you specify as such. 

	--- @param tUser User
	--- @param sData string
	function ChatArrival(tUser, sData)
		Core.SendToAll(tUser.sNick .. sData)
	end

Followed by <leader>pa to save. Put the cursor on the function line and it
inserts one --- @param per argument.

Regenerating
------------

	lua contrib/nvim/gen-api-defs.lua scripting.docs/scripting-interface.txt \
	    > contrib/nvim/lua/pxapi/ptokax-api.lua

Parameter types come from the Hungarian prefix on each name: s string, i and u
integer, b boolean, n number, f function, t table. tUser maps to the User
class. Names the rule cannot type are in the OVERRIDE table near the top.

Return types come from the RETURNS table, keyed Namespace.Function. Anything
not listed falls back to reading the description, which gives boolean? for
"Return nil when failed, true when success", table for "Return table with",
and any otherwise. Add to RETURNS rather than improving the prose reader.


Known gaps
----------

Overloads are emitted in document order, so the first form listed is the one
signature help shows first. Core.GetOnlineUsers has four. 

Functions taking sNick/sIP alternatives are typed from the first name only,
so BanMan.GetBan is string either way.

Nil checking is strict on field access, so Core.GetUser("bob").sNick is an
error until you test the result. It is lenient about passing a string? into a
string parameter (LuaLS behavior).
